    "['cablelength [parameter1] [parameter2]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ct1-1/0/0 cable-length long 0db', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces e1-1/0/0'], 'root': 'set interfaces e1-1/0/0', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset interfaces ct1-1/0/0 cable-length long 0db\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, the controller configuration is typically done under the physical interface (ct1 for T1/E1 interfaces). The \"long 0db\" parameter remains the same as it\\'s a standard E1 cable specification. The interface naming follows Juniper\\'s convention where \"ct1\" indicates a T1/E1 controller interface.', 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ct1-1/0/0 cable-length long 0db'}]]": 1,
    "['channel-group [parameter1] timeslots [parameter2]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces e1-1/0/0 channel-group 1 timeslots 1-31', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces e1-1/0/0'], 'root': 'set interfaces e1-1/0/0', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco `channel-group` command:\\n\\n##\\nset interfaces e1-1/0/0 channel-group 1 timeslots 1-31\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, the interface naming convention is slightly different, using hyphens instead of slashes (e1-1/0/0 instead of E1 1/0/0). The command structure is otherwise similar, maintaining the same channel group number and timeslot range.\", 'trans_template': 'set interfaces e1-1/0/0 channel-group 1 timeslots 1-31'}]]": 1,
    "['no shutdown', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces multilink1 disable', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces e1-1/0/0'], 'root': 'set interfaces e1-1/0/0', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset interfaces multilink1 disable\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, \"no shutdown\" in Cisco translates to enabling the interface, which is the default state. To disable an interface in Juniper, you would use \"set interfaces <name> disable\". However, since \"no shutdown\" means to enable the interface in Cisco, the direct equivalent in Juniper would be to not include any disable statement (as interfaces are enabled by default). \\n\\nIf you specifically need to represent the \"enable\" state in Juniper (though not normally required), you could use:\\n##\\ndelete interfaces multilink1 disable\\n##\\n\\nBut the most accurate translation for \"no shutdown\" (enable) is simply not having a \"disable\" statement in Juniper. The first answer shows the inverse for clarity.', 'trans_template': 'set interfaces multilink1 disable'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'delete interfaces multilink1 disable', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces e1-1/0/0'], 'root': 'set interfaces e1-1/0/0', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset interfaces multilink1 disable\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, \"no shutdown\" in Cisco translates to enabling the interface, which is the default state. To disable an interface in Juniper, you would use \"set interfaces <name> disable\". However, since \"no shutdown\" means to enable the interface in Cisco, the direct equivalent in Juniper would be to not include any disable statement (as interfaces are enabled by default). \\n\\nIf you specifically need to represent the \"enable\" state in Juniper (though not normally required), you could use:\\n##\\ndelete interfaces multilink1 disable\\n##\\n\\nBut the most accurate translation for \"no shutdown\" (enable) is simply not having a \"disable\" statement in Juniper. The first answer shows the inverse for clarity.', 'trans_template': 'delete interfaces multilink1 disable'}]]": 1,
